<h1>Chat Panel</h1>

<p>
    The Chat Panel is your communication hub in Quickfire. It sits alongside your work so you can stay in context while you communicate with team members, website visitors, and clients via text message.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Full-page screenshot showing the chat panel on the right side of the screen with three tabs visible: Staff, Web, and SMS. Show unread badges on the tabs. Include the main content area on the left to show how chat stays in context. Use labels to identify each tab. -->

<h2>Three Channels, One Place</h2>

<p>
    The Chat Panel has three separate channels, each for a different type of communication:
</p>

<h3>Staff Chat</h3>

<p>
    Staff chat is for internal team communication. Use it for:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Quick questions and answers between team members</li>
    <li>Internal notes and discussions</li>
    <li>Fast back-and-forth without leaving Quickfire</li>
    <li>Sharing information while working on clients or renewals</li>
</ul>

<p>
    Staff chat keeps conversations in Quickfire so you don't need to switch to email or other messaging apps for internal communication.
</p>

<h3>Web Chat</h3>

<p>
    Web chat shows messages from visitors on your website in real time. This is one of Quickfire's most powerful features:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Real-time communication</strong> – See messages as visitors type them on your website</li>
    <li><strong>Context preservation</strong> – When a web chat closes, the transcript links back to the client record (if they're a client)</li>
    <li><strong>Seamless integration</strong> – Reply directly from Quickfire without leaving your work</li>
    <li><strong>Full history</strong> – All web chat conversations are saved and searchable</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>How it works:</strong> Visitors to your website can start a chat through a chat widget. Their messages appear in your Web Chat tab in real time. You can reply, and they'll see your response on the website. When the chat ends, if the visitor is (or becomes) a client, the entire conversation is linked to their client record.
</p>

<h3>SMS Chat</h3>

<p>
    SMS chat keeps your text message threads alongside everything else so you can:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Reply to text messages without leaving Quickfire</li>
    <li>See SMS conversations alongside other communications</li>
    <li>Keep all client communication in one place</li>
    <li>Access text message history easily</li>
</ul>

<p>
    Messages stay in context with the client record so you can review the full history in one place.
</p>

